{"index":3303,"node_id":"4896ebbb-08df-4697-a8ae-e548820f1a57","parent":"ebabfa63-b2e4-48d3-8909-030d04ee5ff0","role":"assistant","author_name":null,"create_time":1791159460.349556,"update_time":1791159480.774391,"content_type":"text","status":"finished_successfully","end_turn":true,"recipient":"all","model_slug":"gpt-5-6-thinking","default_model_slug":"gpt-5-6-thinking","part":1,"parts_total":1,"text":"Implemented.\n\nEach report `.txt` now has a **fixed 16,384-character metadata region** at the start of the file. I used `~` as the recurring non-space padding character.\n\nThe metadata itself tracks the reservation:\n\n```json\n\"metadata_region\": {\n  \"length\": 16384,\n  \"padding_character\": \"~\",\n  \"body_start\": 16384,\n  \"coordinate_unit\": \"UTF-16_code_unit\"\n}\n```\n\nThe first document confirms that structure is present. fileciteturn800file0L2-L5\n\nThe physical layout is:\n\n```text\n{metadata JSON}\\n\n~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~...~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~\\n\nBODY STARTS HERE\n```\n\nThe entire first region—including the JSON, two newline characters, and `~` padding—is always exactly **16,384 characters**. Therefore ordinary metadata growth or shrinkage changes only the number of `~` characters; it does **not** move the body.\n\n`append-to-metadata.js` was also revised accordingly: it extracts the body at `metadata_region.body_start`, modifies the metadata, recalculates the padding, verifies the reserved region remains exactly the declared length, and then reattaches the body unchanged. fileciteturn811file0L2-L5\n\nThe migration workflow converted the existing documents and completed successfully. fileciteturn804file0L2-L5 The manifest workflow now validates the reserved-region structure and also completed successfully across the report documents. fileciteturn808file0L2-L5\n\nCurrent `main` is:\n\n`671a82f48a181db8e5926af7a28fcd3dfe338490` fileciteturn809file0L2-L5"}
